endobj
157 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E (CETA places strong emphasis on innovation, providing a boost for sectors such as AI, deep tech, space technology and defence innovation. The deal strengthens government, industry, university and research collaboration through R&D, capacity building, technical cooperation and technology transfer, overseen by a new Innovation Working Group. Government procurement provisions also open UK public-sector digital and AI contracts to Indian bidders more equitably.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
158 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E (Healthcare)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
159 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E (India has had a long history of sending GPs, specialists and medical practitioners within the NHS since its very inception. Healthcare and health-related professional services were both included among India's opened sub-sectors, and the sector benefits disproportionately from the mobility and mutual-recognition provisions. While the agreement in its current form stops short of a full mutual recognition of professional qualifications, CETA does create an opportunity for healthcare professionals in both countries for greater meaningful partnership.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
160 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E (Finance and Investment)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
161 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E (CETA includes a dedicated Financial Services chapter that covers national treatment, most-favoured-nation treatment, market access, and prudential carve-outs. UK-based asset managers, insurers, and banks are likely to have greater legal certainty and continuity of access within Indian markets. The agreement is a foundation for deeper capital-markets engagement at a time that UK investors are actively looking to diversify allocations to India.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
162 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 60 0 R
/T (Brishni Mukhopadhyay)
/E (Brishni Mukhopadhyay)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
163 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E (Legal Services)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
164 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E (While the current iteration of CETA does not explicitly include further access for legal services, market access, trade and bilateral relationships across firms in both countries will result in opportunities for cross-border legal advice. CETA does offer improved transparency and mobility for legal process outsourcing, contract and compliance support services, and dispute-related professional services. Firms advising cross-border clients should treat this as unfinished business rather than a settled outcome.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
165 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E (Education)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
166 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E (Education is one of the sub-sectors that provides opportunities through institutional partnerships and people. There is a clearer route to establish joint programmes, transnational education partnerships, and prospective campus presences in India. The formalisation of institutions to facilitate the exchange of ideas is crucial as students and professors embark on addressing the key challenges confronting society today.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E (CLIFF and CETA)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
168 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E (CLIFF is ideally positioned to shape the dialogue at the college level by bringing together students to address these challenges. As one of the largest and most diverse colleges in terms of student population, Lucy Cavendish through CLIFF has the ability to partner with academia, the public and private sectors and transform students into future leaders who can implement the goals outlined in CETA. There is little doubt that CLIFF and Lucy Cavendish are ideally placed to deliver on the promise of CETA.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
