/P 61 0 R
/E (Care ethics emerged in the early 1980s from feminist critiques of mainstream moral psychology. Its foundational text is Carol Gilligan's In a Different Voice \(1982\), which argued that women)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
184 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (in empirical studies, when faced with moral dilemmas, foregrounded intimate relationships and the significance of caring for others. Since then, care ethics has faced serious challenges, most notably that it valorises the conditions of oppression it should be criticising.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
185 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 61 0 R
/T (Dr Ira Chadha-Sridhar is an Assistant Professor in Law at the University of Warwick and was the Hatton-WYNG Junior Research Fellow in Law, Medicine and Life Sciences at Hughes Hall College. She holds a PhD \(Law\) from King's College and an LLM from Lucy Cavendish College, University of Cambridge. )
/E (Dr Ira Chadha-Sridhar is an Assistant Professor in Law at the University of Warwick and was the Hatton-WYNG Junior Research Fellow in Law, Medicine and Life Sciences at Hughes Hall College. She holds a PhD \(Law\) from King's College and an LLM from Lucy Cavendish College, University of Cambridge. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
186 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (Dr Chadha-Sridhar's research addresses these debates directly. She argues that care should be seen as a "thick ethical concept," combining description and evaluation, and that caring actions can be morally bad in virtue of the fact that they are caring. This allows care ethicists to better address prevalent criticisms.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
187 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
188 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (Her research agenda is most clearly exemplified in her forthcoming monograph, Care and the Mandate of Medical Law, to be published by Oxford University Press in 2027 as part of the Oxford Legal Philosophy series. The book offers a new account of medical negligence law rooted in a close philosophical analysis of the concept that underlies legal doctrine: care. The law of negligence is structured around the idea of care, yet there has been no sustained engagement between negligence doctrine and the ethics of care. The book fills that gap.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
