225 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 63 0 R
/E (Jawaharlal Nehru had come up to Trinity College, Cambridge, in 1907. He read Natural Sciences, graduating in 1910 with a Second. By 1911, he was in London, studying for the Bar at the Inns of Court. He was slim, well-dressed, quietly ambitious, and losing his hair.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
226 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 63 0 R
/E (The letter he wrote to his father Motilal that September is one of the most disarming documents in the Nehru archive. It is not about Indian self-governance. It is not about the Congress. It is about the state of his hair. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 63 0 R
/E (There is something wonderful about this letter. In 1911, the man who would go on to lead India was a young man in his twenties worrying about going bald. He was writing to his father the way any son would: a little plaintively, half-hoping for a solution and half-resigned to the fact that there probably was not one.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 63 0 R
/E (It is a reminder that the people who shaped history were, before anything else, people. They sat in rooms that were too cold, ate food that was not from home, worried about things that did not matter, and wrote letters to their parents about it. Here, young Jawahar was not yet Pandit Nehru. He was a student, he was far from home, and his hair was falling out. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
